1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,787
This demonstration shows MySQL Cluster Set across independent community OpenShift clusters in London and Frankfurt. We first tour

2
00:00:06,787 --> 00:00:13,573
the real consoles, with private fields masked. We then use an illustrative terminal replay to explain creation, replication,

3
00:00:13,573 --> 00:00:20,360
and a controlled switch of the active writer.

4
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:27,920
This is the London OKD console. The MySQL Operator deployment lives in the mysql-operator namespace. This local controller

5
00:00:27,920 --> 00:00:35,480
manages London database resources. It does not manage the Frankfurt Kubernetes API.

6
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:43,740
Frankfurt has its own MySQL Operator in a namespace with the same name. These are independent controllers. The

7
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:52,000
bridge between sites is MySQL Cluster Set replication over reachable database endpoints, rather than one shared Kubernetes controller.

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,480
The Submariner namespace contains its controller and Lighthouse deployments. Submariner gateways and route integration carry cross-cluster traffic. The

9
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:08,960
London broker exchanges endpoint and service metadata. It is not a relay for the MySQL data stream.

10
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:15,000
The Frankfurt console lists the active gateway resource. Its peer connection is checked separately through the Gateway status

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,040
and command line. The gateways use private routing between the regions. A connected tunnel alone does not prove

12
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:27,080
that Lighthouse service records are synchronized.

13
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:33,400
Lighthouse watches Service Exports, then exchanges Service Import and Endpoint Slice metadata through the broker. This London database

14
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:39,720
service is imported into Frankfurt. The source namespace must exist at the destination. Stable pod names include the

15
00:01:39,720 --> 00:01:46,040
Submariner cluster identifier.

16
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:52,693
The OKD DNS Operator forwards the cluster set dot local suffix to the local Lighthouse DNS service. Lighthouse

17
00:01:52,693 --> 00:01:59,347
builds those service answers from synchronized metadata. It does not manage the OCI private DNS zones for the

18
00:01:59,347 --> 00:02:06,000
API, internal API, or application ingress routes.

19
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,373
We now move to the terminal replay. Prerequisites and protected root Secrets already exist. Each region has the

20
00:02:12,373 --> 00:02:18,747
local database namespace, and an empty peer namespace for Lighthouse imports. Names and output in this walkthrough are

21
00:02:18,747 --> 00:02:25,120
examples, not a recording of a new live installation.

22
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:31,667
Create the London InnoDB Cluster using its reviewed manifest. The local operator prepares the database and Router, then

23
00:02:31,667 --> 00:02:38,213
the cluster becomes online. In this Operator workflow, the initial Cluster Set domain is created as part of

24
00:02:38,213 --> 00:02:44,760
bootstrap. We read that existing domain from the center terminal.

25
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:51,053
The headless database service is explicitly exported. Lighthouse synchronizes it into the matching peer namespace. From a Frankfurt

26
00:02:51,053 --> 00:02:57,347
pod, the qualified London member name resolves. The database manifest advertises that stable name, so remote MySQL members

27
00:02:57,347 --> 00:03:03,640
can reach it as well.

28
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:10,467
The Frankfurt manifest uses init DB cluster Set with the primary database target and a local root Secret

29
00:03:10,467 --> 00:03:17,293
reference. The Frankfurt sidecar invokes MySQL Admin API to create the replica cluster and configure its asynchronous channel.

30
00:03:17,293 --> 00:03:24,120
The two Operators continue managing their own local Kubernetes resources.

31
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:30,947
Check each direct database member. London is writable, and Frankfurt has super read only enabled. Each single-member group

32
00:03:30,947 --> 00:03:37,773
has a local primary member, but their global Cluster Set roles differ. A Router in Frankfurt may still

33
00:03:37,773 --> 00:03:44,600
route to the global primary, so direct sessions are used for this verification.

34
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:49,987
In the replay, a demo row is written on the London primary and then read directly on the

35
00:03:49,987 --> 00:03:55,373
Frankfurt replica. This checks database replication over the connected network. DNS success by itself would not prove that

36
00:03:55,373 --> 00:04:00,760
the transaction arrived.

37
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:07,107
We next preview a planned switch of the active cluster to Frankfurt. The dry run verifies the Cluster

38
00:04:07,107 --> 00:04:13,453
Set and target replica without changing roles. Both clusters must be reachable and transaction state must be suitable.

39
00:04:13,453 --> 00:04:19,800
This is a controlled switchover, not emergency forced failover.

40
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:26,373
The replay now executes the planned switchover. MySQL Shell synchronizes the target, coordinates the role change, and updates

41
00:04:26,373 --> 00:04:32,947
the managed replication topology. Router behavior follows Cluster Set metadata and routing policy. The left and right terminals

42
00:04:32,947 --> 00:04:39,520
will now verify the resulting roles.

43
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:45,467
The final role checks are made against each direct database. Frankfurt is now the writable global primary. London

44
00:04:45,467 --> 00:04:51,413
is read only and receives the managed asynchronous stream. ONLINE status must be retained at both sites, and

45
00:04:51,413 --> 00:04:57,360
global Cluster Set health must be healthy.

46
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:03,880
A new transaction on Frankfurt is now read directly on London. This proves the new replication direction in

47
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:10,400
the instructional sequence. Keep the network path, service discovery, local reconciliation, and database replication checks separate when operating

48
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:16,920
the real deployment.

49
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:25,533
The console views in this video were captured read only from the existing deployment and redacted. Creation, SQL

50
00:05:25,533 --> 00:05:34,147
writes, and the Frankfurt switchover were illustrative replay scenes, not live operations. Submariner carries the traffic. Lighthouse provides

51
00:05:34,147 --> 00:05:42,760
service discovery. Each Operator reconciles locally. MySQL Cluster Set controls database replication and global roles.
